{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["And the reason for <b>which we ate in Egypt for free</b>, according to its plain sense, is that the Egyptian fishermen would press them into service to haul in the fish caught in nets and baskets, and they would give them from the fish according to the custom of all those who spread a basket-net. And the cucumbers and the melons and the leeks and the onions and the garlic were exceedingly abundant in Egypt, for it is like a vegetable garden, and when they would dig in the gardens and perform all field labor, they would eat from the vegetables. Or perhaps the Israelites were there as the king's servants performing his work, and he would provision them with meager bread and scant water, and they would scatter through the city and enter the gardens and fields, and they would eat from the vegetables at no cost, as is the way of the king's servants, and along the riverbank they were given, from the king's ration, small fish which have no value in Egypt, as I explained in the section of Shemot (Exodus 1:11). Now this was the complaint of the children of Israel, not the complaint of the rabble, and they were murmuring against Moses and crying out to him, \"Give us meat and we will eat,\" as he mentions further (Numbers 11:13)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase במדבר י״ב:י״ג at the end of some editions should be במדבר י״א:י״ג, as the verse cited (תנה לנו בשר ונאכלה) is Numbers 11:13; rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:16.955041010Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}